Q: Laravel 5 - artisan seed [ReflectionException] Class SongsTableSeeder does not exist A: <p>If our CustomTableSeeder is in same directory with DatabaseSeeder we should use like below:</p>
<pre><code>$this-&gt;call('database\seeds\CustomTableSeeder');
</code></pre>
<p>in our DatabaseSeeder File;
then another error will be thrown that says: 'DB Class not found' .
hen we should add our DB facade to our CustomTableSeeder File like below:</p>
<pre><code>use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
</code></pre>
<p>it worked for me!</p>

Q: How can I restrict a method to only classes implementing a specific interface in Java? A: <h2>Naming issues</h2>
<p>You have some naming issues that, while not the source of the problem you asked about, seem to have caused some confusion here, and might also be a source of confusion or misconception for you.  The most significant of these is your two distinct flavors of <code>heal()</code> methods, where</p>
<ul>
<li><code>GameCharacter.heal(int)</code> expresses the invoker performing healing on the target, whereas</li>
<li><code>Healable.heal(GameCharacter)</code> expresses the invoker instructing the target to perform healing on the argument.</li>
</ul>
<p>These differ in who is the healing actor and who the recipient relative to the method invocation, and that's confusing.</p>
<p>This comes to a head in your <code>Healer</code> class, which has overloaded <code>heal()</code> methods, one of each type.  Plus, the <code>@Override</code> annotation could suggest, incorrectly, that <code>Healer.heal(GameCharacter)</code> overrides <code>GameCharacter.heal(int)</code>, whereas in fact, it overrides (only) <code>Healable.heal(GameCharacter)</code>.</p>
<p>Furthermore, the <code>Xable</code> idiom for naming interfaces is primarily intended and suited for methods <code>X</code> of the former semantic variety, where the invoker is understood as performing X on the target (closing, runnning, ...). This is like <code>GameCharacter.heal(int)</code> but unlike <code>Healer.heal(GameCharacter)</code>, for which you are actually using this idiom.</p>
<h2>Strategy considerations</h2>
<p>You wrote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>According to my game rules, only a Healer should be able to perform a healing action, either on itself or on another character.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is well and good, but it doesn't necessarily need to be enforced by your types.  You always have the option of not programming things that you don't want to happen.  In fact, you're going to have to do that whether your types actually enforce the rule or not, so it does not represent a tradeoff itself.  The tradeoff to consider here is whether it is worthwhile to invest time, effort, and code complexity into baking that rule into your types, in exchange for some extra assistance from the compiler, to the extent that it can provide it, in ensuring that you don't erroneously program something contrary to your rules.</p>
<h2>Possible implementation</h2>
<p>If you want to build your <code>GameCharacter</code> class such that instances can receive healing only from objects of certain classes, then multiple dispatch, a.k.a. the Visitor pattern, seems a pretty good tool.  Instead of a <code>heal(int)</code> method, your <code>GameCharacter</code> would have something like <code>receiveHealingFrom(Healer)</code>.  @MarcePuente already suggested an approach from this direction.  To really seal the deal, though, you could combine this with exposing the healing mechanism via a lambda or an inner class, or by otherwise making the character health a mutable object instead of a plain <code>int</code>. That allows you to both secure access to healing and decouple healing from the details of <code>GameCharacter</code>.</p>
<h3>Preliminaries</h3>
<p>Programming to interfaces instead of to classes is a good practice in complex programs.  You've already started in that direction, but you've gone only halfway.  Let's take the next step:</p>
<pre><code>public interface Healer {
    void heal(Healable);
}

public interface Healable {
    void receiveHealing(int amount);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that between those, healing is fully abstracted.  A <code>Healer</code> can be another character, a potion, an environmental feature, or whatever.  And a <code>Healable</code> can be a character, a pet, an NPC, or anything else you want to allow.</p>
<h3>Double dispatch + lambda</h3>
<p>Here's a way to ensure that there is a <code>Healer</code> in the loop for any character healing, without coupling <code>GameCharacter</code> to the implementation of <code>Healer</code>:</p>
<pre><code>public class GameCharacter {

    private int maximumHealth;
    private int health;

    public void receiveHealingFrom(Healer healer) {
        healer.heal((amount) -&gt; {
            // this health and maximumHealth are those of the containing instance:
            health = Math.min(health + amount, maximumHealth);
        });
    }

    // ...
}

public class HealerCharacter extends GameCharacter implements Healer {
    int healingPower;

    public void heal(Healable target) {
        target.receiveHealing(healingPower);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Observe that that does not restrict what objects can invoke <code>GameCharacter.receiveHealingFrom()</code>.  You probably don't actually want to restrict that to healers anyway -- I expect that you will actually want the game engine to invoke it, instead.  However, if you put <code>GameCharacter</code> in the same package as (the rest of) the game engine then you could consider removing the <code>public</code> access.  Either way, it does force healing to involve a <code>Healer</code>, provided that you don't create any other methods through which healing can be effected.</p>

Q: ASP.NET : Form posting via jQuery A: <p>you can use simple jQuery selectors to select Server Side Controls suppose you have following textbox on your page:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;asp:TextBox id="userName" runat="server" /&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>in jQuery you can access it like this:</p>

<pre><code>var valueOfTextBox = $("input[id$='userName']").val();
//OR
var valueOfTextBox = $("input[id$='userName']").attr("value");
</code></pre>

<p>if you have few controls on page then this technique is okay but for large forms please use this instead:</p>

<pre><code>var valueOfTextBox = $("#&lt;%= userName.ClientID %&gt;").val();
//OR
var valueOfTextBox = $("#&lt;%= userName.ClientID %&gt;").attr("value");
</code></pre>

<p>There is huge performance gain, as explained by Dave <a href="http://encosia.com/2009/06/09/11-keystrokes-that-made-my-jquery-selector-run-10x-faster/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>

<p>You can find more about jQuery selector syntax <a href="http://docs.jquery.com/Selectors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>

<p>If you want to post form via jquery AJAX then use this code:</p>

<pre><code>  $("#form1").submit(function(){//Where form1 is your form's id
    var a=$("input[id$='userName']").val();
    var b=$("input[id$='userId']").val();
    $.ajax({
       type: "POST",
       url: "yourPage.aspx",
       data: "name=" + a+"&amp;id=" + b,
       success: function(msg){
         alert( "Data Saved: " + msg );
       }
     });
  });
</code></pre>

<p>More details about jQuery AJAX are <a href="http://docs.jquery.com/Ajax/jQuery.ajax#options" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>

<p>You can also use <a href="http://malsup.com/jquery/form/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jquery.form</a> plugin to automate the things.</p>

Q: How to preview the changes that &quot;git stash apply&quot; will make? A: <p>I use this to see all my stashes with colour diff highlighting (on Fedora 21):</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>git stash list | 
  awk -F: '{ print &quot;



&quot;; print $0; print &quot;

&quot;; 
  system(&quot;git -c color.ui=always stash show -p &quot; $1); }' | 
  less -R
</code></pre>
<p>(Adapted from <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10725729/git-see-whats-in-a-stash-without-applying-stash#comment27975633_10726185">See what&#39;s in a stash without applying it</a>)</p>
<p>Or the <em>one-liner</em> version:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>git stash list | awk -F: '{ print &quot;



&quot;; print $0; print &quot;

&quot;; system(&quot;git -c color.ui=always stash show -p &quot; $1); }' | less -R
</code></pre>
<p>And here is the <code>alias</code> version:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>alias git-stash-view=&quot;git stash list | awk -F: '{ print \&quot;



\&quot;; print \$0; print \&quot;

\&quot;; system(\&quot;git -c color.ui=always stash show -p \&quot; \$1); }' | less -R&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: AngularJS and Laravel 4 routing conflict in HTML5 mode A: <p>For Laravel 4.x or 5.x i use this simple and nice trick and there is no need to change <code>.htaccess</code> file. this trick is very simple  and works for me. it doesn't redirect URL and user will stay on same URL when refresh page and page will refresh:</p>
<pre><code>Route::get('dashboard/{all?}', function(){
    return view('main.index');
});
</code></pre>
<p>Here my dashboard app is my SPA. With this solution we can resolve 404 errors pages, too.</p>

Q: PHPExcel export HTML table to xlsx A: <pre><code>&lt;?php
if (function_exists('mb_internal_encoding')) {
    $oldEncoding=mb_internal_encoding();
    mb_internal_encoding('latin1');
}

require_once 'addons/PHPExcel/PHPExcel/IOFactory.php'; //Update your Path for PHPExcel Class
$objPHPExcel = new PHPExcel();

$sheet = $objPHPExcel-&gt;getActiveSheet();
$sheet-&gt;setTitle(&quot;Template Title&quot;);

$sheet-&gt;getColumnDimension('A')-&gt;setWidth(30);

$sheet-&gt;SetCellValue('A1', 'hello world!');

$objWriter = PHPExcel_IOFactory::createWriter($objPHPExcel, 'Excel2007');

if (function_exists('mb_internal_encoding')){
    mb_internal_encoding($oldEncoding); 
}

header('Content-Type: application/vnd.ms-excel');
header(&quot;Content-Disposition: attachment;filename=helloworld.xlsx&quot;);
header('Cache-Control: max-age=0');

$objWriter-&gt;save('php://output');
?&gt;
</code></pre>
